{"assets":[],"contentKind":"statement","contestId":"54","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"attrs":{"level":1,"role":"title"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"C. First Digit Law"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"role":"time_limit"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"time limit per test"},{"kind":"text","text":":"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":"2 seconds"}],"kind":"container"},{"attrs":{"role":"memory_limit"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"memory limit per test"},{"kind":"text","text":":"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":"256 megabytes"}],"kind":"container"},{"attrs":{"role":"body"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In the probability theory the following paradox called Benford's law is known: \"In many lists of random numbers taken from real sources, numbers starting with digit 1 occur much more often than numbers starting with any other digit\" (that's the simplest form of the law)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Having read about it on Codeforces, the Hedgehog got intrigued by the statement and wishes to thoroughly explore it. He finds the following similar problem interesting in particular: there are "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"N"},{"kind":"text","text":" random variables, the "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"i"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th of which can take any integer value from some segment "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"[Li;Ri]"},{"kind":"text","text":" (all numbers from this segment are equiprobable). It means that the value of the "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"i"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th quantity can be equal to any integer number from a given interval "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"[Li;Ri]"},{"kind":"text","text":" with probability "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"1 / (Ri - Li + 1)"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The Hedgehog wants to know the probability of the event that the first digits of at least "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"K%"},{"kind":"text","text":" of those values will be equal to one. In other words, let us consider some set of fixed values of these random variables and leave only the first digit (the MSD — most significant digit) of each value. Then let's count how many times the digit 1 is encountered and if it is encountered in at least "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"K"},{"kind":"text","text":" per cent of those "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"N"},{"kind":"text","text":" values, than such set of values will be called a good one. You have to find the probability that a set of values of the given random variables will be a good one."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"input_specification"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Input"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The first line contains number "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"N"},{"kind":"text","text":" which is the number of random variables ("},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"1 ≤ N ≤ 1000"},{"kind":"text","text":"). Then follow "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"N"},{"kind":"text","text":" lines containing pairs of numbers "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"Li, Ri"},{"kind":"text","text":", each of whom is a description of a random variable. It is guaranteed that "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"1 ≤ Li ≤ Ri ≤ 1018"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The last line contains an integer "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"K"},{"kind":"text","text":" ("},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"0 ≤ K ≤ 100"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"All the numbers in the input file are integers."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Please, do not use "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"%lld"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":" specificator to read or write 64-bit integers in C++. It is preffered to use "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"cin"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":" (also you may use "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"%I64d"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"output_specification"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Print the required probability. Print the fractional number with such a precision that the relative or absolute error of the result won't exceed "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"10 - 9"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"samples"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Examples"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample"},"children":[{"attrs":{"role":"sample_input"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Input"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"1\n1 2\n50\n"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample_output"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"0.500000000000000"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample"},"children":[{"attrs":{"role":"sample_input"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Input"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"2\n1 2\n9 11\n50\n"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample_output"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"0.833333333333333"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"document"},"index":"C","problemCode":"54C","schema":2,"sourceKind":"html","sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/contest/54/problem/C"}